Give the execveat check bail-outs a return value

The only-scripts-verified-by-execveat patch adds two bare `return;'
statements to open_shell_script(), which is declared to return int.
gcc only warns (-Wreturn-type) so the default toolchain build passes,
but clang makes "non-void function should return a value" a hard
error, breaking every clang/lld build (qemu-aarch64-clang sample):
bash 5.2.15 do_compile fails on shell.c:1581/1594.

Return 0 instead: the single caller ignores the return value, so this
only turns gcc's indeterminate value into a defined one - no behavior
change.

--- a/shell.c
+++ b/shell.c
@@ -1578,7 +1578,7 @@
 
   fd = open (filename, O_RDONLY);
   if (mayexec(filename, fd))
-      return;
+      return 0;
   if ((fd < 0) && (errno == ENOENT) && (absolute_program (filename) == 0))
     {
       e = errno;
@@ -1591,7 +1591,7 @@
 	  filename = path_filename;
 	  fd = open (filename, O_RDONLY);
       if (mayexec(filename, fd))
-          return;
+          return 0;
 	}
       else
 	errno = e;
